4.1 BBVACommon (Tier 0)
MustChange / Rewrite
RPL-FR-14 - Protect decrypted HSM credentials in transit (Blocker)
• Category / severity: MustChange/rewrite - Blocker
• Evidence: HSM_Common.cs (../../CMS/replicas/BBVACommon/HSM_Common.cs#L65-L76), ProcesoServicio.cs (../../CMS/replicas/AplicacionReplicas/Servicios/ProcesoServicio.cs#L1504-L1520)
Code block (csharp)
Socket clientSocket = new Socket(AddressFamily.InterNetwork, SocketType.Stream, ProtocolType.Tcp);
clientSocket.Connect(ipHsm, puertoHsm);
clientSocket.Send(bytesCommand);
clientSocket.Receive(bytesReceived);
The service asks the HSM to decrypt the PFX password and digital key, then carries the plaintext response over raw TCP with no peer authentication or channel protection. A network observer or active intermediary can capture or alter both credentials.
Recommendation: use the vendor-supported authenticated protected protocol, or an approved mutually authenticated tunnel with strict network segmentation and documented residual risk. Keep decrypted material inside the smallest possible process scope.
Benefit: prevents credential disclosure or substitution on the HSM network path.
RPL-FR-15 - Never include decrypted HSM payloads in exceptions or logs (Blocker)
• Category / severity: MustChange/rewrite - Blocker
• Evidence: HSM_Common.cs (../../CMS/replicas/BBVACommon/HSM_Common.cs#L104-L113), ProcesoServicio.cs (../../CMS/replicas/AplicacionReplicas/Servicios/ProcesoServicio.cs#L1535-L1538)
On a declared-length mismatch, HSM_Common appends the full decrypted result to the exception; the caller logs the complete exception. A malformed or truncated response can therefore persist a PFX password or digital key in application logs.
Recommendation: report only the response code, expected/actual lengths, and a correlation ID. Review existing logs and rotate affected secrets if this path occurred.
Benefit: removes a high-impact secret-retention path while preserving diagnostic value.
RPL-FR-16 - Implement complete HSM TCP framing (Major)
• Category / severity: MustChange/rewrite - Major
• Evidence: HSM_Common.cs (../../CMS/replicas/BBVACommon/HSM_Common.cs#L10-L76)
One Send and one Receive are assumed to transfer a full frame into a fixed 2,048-byte buffer. TCP permits partial writes/reads, and a valid larger response is truncated before parsing.
Recommendation: loop until the request is fully sent, read and validate the fixed header first, enforce a documented maximum, then read exactly the declared payload length.
Benefit: prevents valid responses from becoming corrupt and blocks oversized/malformed frame ambiguity.
RPL-FR-17 - Add HSM deadlines and cancellation (Major)
• Category / severity: MustChange/rewrite - Major
• Evidence: HSM_Common.cs (../../CMS/replicas/BBVACommon/HSM_Common.cs#L68-L76), ProcesoServicio.cs (../../CMS/replicas/AplicacionReplicas/Servicios/ProcesoServicio.cs#L1504-L1520)
Synchronous Connect, Send, and Receive have no configured timeout or cancellation. A stalled HSM can block a service tick and shutdown indefinitely.
Recommendation: expose a cancellation-aware asynchronous HSM client with bounded connect, send, and receive deadlines propagated from the host.
Benefit: makes tick and shutdown duration bounded under network failure.
RPL-FR-18 - Define and enforce the complete TSEC timeout (Major)
• Category / severity: MustChange/rewrite - Major
• Evidence: ProcesoServicio.cs (../../CMS/replicas/AplicacionReplicas/Servicios/ProcesoServicio.cs#L1528-L1532), Funciones.cs (../../CMS/replicas/BBVACommon/Funciones.cs#L42-L79)
The fallback 5 is assigned directly to HttpWebRequest.Timeout, whose unit is milliseconds, while the synchronous response-body read keeps a separate longer default because ReadWriteTimeout is not set.
Recommendation: define the configuration unit, convert once, validate a safe range, and apply one cancellation/deadline budget across connect, write, headers, and body reads using a modern async HTTP client.
Benefit: avoids both accidental 5 ms failures and response reads that outlive the intended deadline.
RPL-FR-19 - Dispose imported certificates and exceptional request streams (Major)
• Category / severity: MustChange/rewrite - Major
• Evidence: Funciones.cs (../../CMS/replicas/BBVACommon/Funciones.cs#L34-L68)
Imported X509Certificate2 objects are attached to each request but never disposed. The request stream is manually closed after Flush, so a flush exception skips cleanup.
Recommendation: give certificates an explicit owned lifetime and dispose them in finally; wrap the request stream in using so cleanup is unconditional.
Benefit: prevents private-key/certificate handle accumulation in a long-running service.
RPL-FR-20 - Transmit the normalized BBVA contract number (Major)
• Category / severity: MustChange/rewrite - Major
• Evidence: Funciones.cs (../../CMS/replicas/BBVACommon/Funciones.cs#L112-L123)
The method validates contractNumberToSend.Trim().Length, then pads and sends the original untrimmed value. Leading/trailing spaces can pass validation but produce invalid authentication fields.
Recommendation: normalize once, validate the normalized value against the full allowed format, and map only that value.
Benefit: removes configuration-whitespace authentication failures.
RPL-FR-21 - Reject null TSEC requests and empty key material (Minor)
• Category / severity: MustChange/rewrite - Minor
• Evidence: Funciones.cs (../../CMS/replicas/BBVACommon/Funciones.cs#L38-L38), Funciones.cs (../../CMS/replicas/BBVACommon/Funciones.cs#L112-L124)
The request object can serialize as JSON null, and the digital key is added without null/empty validation. Invalid HSM output can therefore become an outbound authentication request.
Recommendation: fail at the boundary with argument/configuration exceptions until request, contract, key, certificate path/password, URI, and timeout are valid.
Benefit: makes malformed security requests deterministic and locally diagnosable.
